# Build Concurrency

**No more than one build runs for a given repo at the same time**, counted across every
session, every worktree of that repo, and every agent. A worktree is the same repo: it shares
the `.git` directory, builds against the same source history, and the same daemon-contention and
output-corruption risks described below apply to it exactly as they do to the main checkout.
Two different repos building at once is fine; the limit is per repo, not machine-wide.

## What counts as a build

Anything that compiles, packages or runs a build tool's test task:

- `gradlew` / `gradle` (assemble, test, install scripts that build)
- `mvn` package, install, verify, test, compile
- `dotnet build`, `dotnet publish`, `dotnet test`, `msbuild`
- `npm` / `yarn` / `pnpm run build`, `cargo build`, `cargo test`

An idle Gradle or Kotlin daemon is **not** a build; it is a process waiting for one. A running
`gradlew` keeps a small wrapper client JVM alive for exactly as long as its build runs,
so that client is what gets counted. Current wrappers start it as
`java -jar ...\gradle\wrapper\gradle-wrapper.jar`, older ones by the `GradleWrapperMain` class name,
so the check matches both. The first version matched only the class name and counted **zero**
while a build was plainly running: always test the check against a build you know is live.

**A shell running the count-and-wait loop below is not a build either, and must never match
the pattern.** The first version of this rule's check counted anything with a matching command
line, with no name filter. An agent waiting for its turn ran the loop in a `bash.exe` subshell;
nothing distinguished that subshell's own command line from a real build in the process list it
was reading, and on at least one run two such wait-loop shells were found still sitting there
after their condition had already been satisfied. Exclude shell process names explicitly
(`bash.exe`, `sh.exe`, `powershell.exe`, `pwsh.exe`, `cmd.exe`, `conhost.exe`) rather than relying
on the command-line pattern alone to miss them.

## Before starting a build

Count the builds already running **for this repo**, including its worktrees:

```powershell
$shells = 'bash.exe', 'sh.exe', 'powershell.exe', 'pwsh.exe', 'cmd.exe', 'conhost.exe'
$pattern = 'gradle-wrapper\.jar|GradleWrapperMain|org\.gradle\.launcher\.GradleMain|' +
    '\bmvn(\.cmd)?"?\s.*\b(package|install|verify|test|compile)\b|' +
    'dotnet(\.exe)?"?\s+(build|publish|test)\b|\bmsbuild(\.exe)?\b|' +
    '\b(npm|yarn|pnpm)(\.cmd)?"?\s+run\s+build\b|cargo(\.exe)?"?\s+(build|test)\b'
$builds = @(Get-CimInstance Win32_Process | Where-Object {
    $_.CommandLine -and $_.Name -notin $shells -and $_.CommandLine -match $pattern })

# Scope to this repo: its shared .git directory is the same for every worktree of it.
$mine = (git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>$null) -replace '\\', '/'
$inRepo = @($builds | Where-Object {
    if ($_.CommandLine -match '-jar\s+"?([^"]+?)[\\/]gradle[\\/]wrapper[\\/]gradle-wrapper\.jar') {
        $root = $Matches[1]
        ((git -C $root rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>$null) -replace '\\', '/') -eq $mine
    } else { $false }
})
$inRepo.Count
```

The Gradle case is shown; the same `git -C <root> rev-parse --git-common-dir` scoping applies to
a Maven, dotnet, npm or cargo invocation by substituting that tool's own way of naming its
project root from its command line (working directory, `-p`/`--project` flag, or similar).

- **0:** start the build.
- **1 or more:** do not start. Wait, checking again every minute or so, and say that you are
  waiting and why. **Never stop another session's build to make room.** It is somebody's
  work, and a killed build can leave a corrupted cache or a half-written output that the next
  build trusts.

## Parallel agents

Launching several agents on the same repo is fine; letting more than one of them build at once
is not.

- **At most one agent builds this repo at a time.** Tell every other agent in its prompt to
  write code and run pure checks only (for example a Python `--selftest`), and to leave building
  to whichever agent currently holds the slot.
- Build and test worktree branches one after another, not concurrently, even though each sits in
  its own directory: they still share the repo's daemons, disk cache and the device the result
  gets installed on.
- The main session's own builds of this repo count towards the limit.
- Agents on **different** repos may each build at the same time; the limit does not stack across
  repos.
- A single repo may set a stricter limit in its own `CLAUDE.md` or memory. The stricter limit
  wins.

## Gradle daemons are shared: no `--stop`, and `--no-daemon` while agents run

A Gradle daemon belongs to the machine user and the Gradle version, not to a checkout. Every
worktree of a repo, and every other repo on the same wrapper version, uses the same daemon
registry under `GRADLE_USER_HOME`, and `gradlew --stop` stops every daemon of that version,
whichever directory started it (details in `jvm-daemon-cleanup`). So a `--stop` in one worktree
kills a build running in another, and the per-repo slot above does not protect it.

Seen Nov-14-2026 building Helm (`D:\GitHub\mobile-ai`) with parallel workflow agents, each in its
own worktree: one agent ran `gradlew --stop`, and another agent's build died with
`Gradle build daemon has been stopped: stop command received`.

While more than one agent or session may be building on the machine:

- **Never run `gradlew --stop`**, and never kill `java` processes, from an agent. Say so in every
  agent's brief.
- **Build with a single-use daemon and capped resources:**

  ```powershell
  .\gradlew.bat --no-daemon --max-workers=2 "-Dorg.gradle.jvmargs=-Xmx1536m" <tasks>
  ```

  `--no-daemon` gives the build its own JVM that no other build can stop or wedge. The worker
  and heap caps keep overlapping builds (other repos, or more than one per repo when the user
  has explicitly allowed it) inside the machine's memory. Keep the `-D` argument quoted in
  PowerShell, or it is split at the `=`.
- **Size the overlap to the machine.** The Helm machine (8 CPUs, 16 GB RAM) carried three
  Android builds at once with these flags. Treat three as the ceiling for a machine that size,
  not as a target.

## Why

On Sep-26-2026, three worktree agents and the main session all built the same Android repo at
once:

- A release build **failed outright**. The install script then put the previous APK on the
  phone, so a device test measured old code and reported a fix as not working.
- Builds that normally take two minutes took **6 to 14**.
- One Gradle daemon and one Kotlin daemon held **about 4.5 GB** between them.

The user stopped every build and set a two-builds-machine-wide limit. On Oct-02-2026, a six-group
worktree Workflow against the same repo exposed the next problem with that version of the rule:
the machine-wide count had no concept of "repo" at all, so an agent whose own repo's slot was
free could still be blocked by an unrelated build in a different repo, and a wait-loop shell
polling the check could itself get miscounted as a build (see above), leaving the agent stuck
waiting on a condition that was already true. The rule moved to one build per repo, counted
across that repo's worktrees, with shells excluded from the process match by name rather than by
pattern alone. Builds on one machine still compete for CPU, memory, disk and daemon locks; a
repo-scoped limit keeps that competition to builds that actually share a cache and a device,
instead of throttling unrelated work for no reason.

See also the `jvm-daemon-cleanup` skill for reclaiming memory from idle daemons, and for telling
an idle daemon apart from a live build, and the `agent-orchestration-fit` skill for creating
worktrees for parallel agents.
